      "math_academy_id": "q-39041",
      "sequence_position": 3,
      "problem": "Given that $x$ is a factor of $f(x)=-{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}-4x,$ which of the following curves is generated by $y=f(x)?$\n\n(*Note: Figures not drawn to scale*)",
      "worked_solution": "First, let's factor the polynomial. We factor out a common factor $-x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =-{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}-4x \\\\  & =-x({x}^{2}+2x+4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation is now fully factored. Note that the discriminant of ${x}^{2}+2x+4=0$ is \n\n$$\nD={2}^{2}-4(1)(4)=-12<0,\n$$\n\nwhich implies that the quadratic factor has no real roots. So by the zero-product rule, we deduce that $f(x)$ has a single root at $x=0.$ \n\nWe also find the $y$-intercept of the curve by substituting the value $x=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\ny=-{0}^{3}-2(0)^{2}-4(0)=0\n$$\n\nSo, the curve:\n\n- has a $y$-intercept at $(0,0),$\n\n- passes through the $x$-axis at the single root $x=0,$ and\n\n- has no more $x$-intercepts.\n\nBecause the leading coefficient is negative, the graph has an $\\text{S}$-shape. Therefore, the graph looks as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14251864/assets/f621534a395f8b0692d31bb3b491b0237877e6dc3ea94a2299d354857c7c3bfd.png)",
      "answer_fields_complete": true,
      "result": "Correct",
      "grade": "Correct",
